# Frontend conventions

## Users

Compliance agents with varying technical skill. Keep copy plain. Primary actions visible on the main surface — do not hide verify/upload/review in nested menus.

## Visual system

Follow `docs/UI_DESIGN.md` and ADR-021:

- Treasury-inspired institutional navy / blue / gold tokens from `index.css`
- No official Treasury seal or agency insignia
- No purple AI branding, gradients, glassmorphism, sparkle/robot imagery
- Semantic PASS / REVIEW / FAIL colors with text + icon
- Reuse `AppShell`, `StatusBadge`, and `.lv-*` component classes for Batch Review later

## Structure

- `components/upload` — intake
- `components/verification` — results and explanations
- `components/batch` — batch flows (not implemented)
- `components/common` — shared UI (`AppShell`, mark, status)
- `services` — backend HTTP client only (no secrets)
- `types` — API-aligned models including PASS/REVIEW/FAIL

## Security

Never add OpenAI keys or other secrets to Vite env. All AI calls stay on the backend.

## Explainability

Surfaces must be able to show application vs detected values, status, explanation, decision method, and processing time when the API provides them. Keep technical OCR/AI details in disclosures.

## File headers

Every `.ts`/`.tsx` file starts with a short comment describing purpose and architectural responsibility.
